To solve this problem, we first need to expand the determinant of the given 3x3 matrix. We can use cofactor expansion along any row or column. Let's choose to expand along the first row for simplicity.

## Step 2: Apply the formula for expanding a 3x3 determinant
Using the formula for expanding a 3x3 determinant, we get:
\begin{align*}
&1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} x & y \\ y & x \end{vmatrix} - 4 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} 3 & y \\ 3 & x \end{vmatrix} + 9 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} 3 & x \\ 3 & y \end{vmatrix}\\
&=1(x^2-y^2) - 4(3x-3y) + 9(3y-3x)
\end{align*}

## Step 3: Simplify the expression obtained from expanding the determinant
We simplify the expression:
\begin{align*}
&(x^2-y^2) -12(x-y) +27(y-x)\\
&= x^2-y^2 -12x+12y-27x+27y\\
&= x^2 - 39x + y^2 + 39y
\end{align*}

## Step 4: Set the simplified expression equal to zero and rearrange terms
Since we are given that the determinant equals zero, we set our simplified expression equal to zero:
\begin{align*}
&x^2 - 39x + y^2 + 39y = 0\\
&(x^2 - 39x) + (y^2+39y)=0
\end{align*}

