& = 22.5^\circ
\end{align*}

## Step 2
Since $\triangle ABC \cong \triangle ADC$ and $DC = BA$, we also have that the corresponding angles of $\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle ADC$ are congruent.
\begin{align*}
m\angle BAC & = m\angle CAD\\
& = 22.5^\circ
\end{align*}

## Step 3
We know that $DC = BA$, so the corresponding angles of $\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle ADC$ are congruent, specifically,
$\begin{aligned}m\angle BCA & = m\angle ACD\\&=22.5^{\circ}\end{aligned}$

## Step 4
We can use this to find the measure of angle $ABC$ by subtracting the measures of angles $BAC$ and $BCA$ from $180^\circ$. We know that $\triangle ABC$ is a triangle, so its interior angles must add up to $180^\circ$.
\begin{align*}
m\angle ABC & = 180^\circ - m\angle BAC - m\angle BCA\\
& = 180^\circ - 22.5^\circ - 22.5^\circ
\end{align*}

## Step 5
Now, we just need to do the arithmetic to find the measure of angle $ABC$.
\begin{align*}
m\angle ABC & = 180^\circ - 45^\circ\\
&= \boxed{135^\circ}
\end{align*}

The final answer is: $\boxed{135}$

