The area of the garden was 500 square feet$(50\times 10)$ and its perimeter was 120 feet, $2\times (50+10)$. The square garden is also enclosed by 120 feet of fence so its sides are each 30 feet long. The square garden's area is 900 square feet ($30\times 30)$. and this has increased the garden area by $\boxed{400}$ square feet. [asy]
/* AMC8 1999 #5 Solution*/
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,50)--(0,50)--cycle);
draw((25,20)--(55,20)--(55,50)--(25,50)--cycle);
label("50", (0, 25), W);
label("10", (5, 0), S);
label("30", (40, 20), S);
label("30", (25, 35), W);
[/asy]

llama_partial_cot_response:
To continue the reasoning:

3. Since the perimeter of a square is given by P = 4s, where s is the length of a side, we can find the length of a side of the new square garden:
\begin{align*}
P_{\text{square}} &= P_{\text{rectangular}}\\
4s &= 120\\
s &= \frac{120}{4} = 30
\end{align*}

Now that we have found the length of a side of the new square garden, we can find its area:
\begin{align*}
\text{Area}_{\text{square}} &= s^2 \\
&= 30^2 \\
&= 900 \text{ square feet}
\end{align*}

To find the enlargement of the garden, we subtract the area of the original rectangular garden from the area of the new square garden:
\begin{align*}
\text{Enlargement} &= \text{Area}_{\text{square}} - \text{Area}_{\text{rectangular}} \\
&= 900 - 500 \\
&= 400
\end{align*}

Therefore, the enlargement of the garden is $\boxed{400}$ square feet.

