To solve this problem, we need to apply the function $f(x)$ three times to the input $x=2$. We start by calculating $f(2)$:

\begin{align*}
f(2) &= 5 \cdot 2 - 4 \\
&= 10 - 4 \\
&= 6
\end{align*}

Next, we calculate $f(f(2)) = f(6)$:

\begin{align*}
f(6) &= 5 \cdot 6 - 4 \\
&= 30 - 4 \\
&= 26
\end{align*}

Finally, we calculate $f(f(f(2))) = f(26)$:

\begin{align*}
f(26) &= 5 \cdot 26 - 4 \\
&= 130 - 4 \\
&= 126
\end{align*}

Therefore, the final answer is: $\boxed{126}$

