When solving this problem, we must remember to apply the order of operations. First, we perform all cases of multiplication and division from left to right to obtain

\begin{align*}
1+6\cdot2-3+5\cdot4\div2&=1+12-3+5\cdot4\div2\\
&=1+12-3+20\div 2\\
&=1+12-3+10.
\end{align*}Next, we perform all the required additions and subtractions from left to right. We get  \begin{align*}
1+12-3+10&=13-3+10\\
&=10+10\\
&=\boxed{20}.
\end{align*}

llama_direct_raw:
\begin{align*}
5^{a_{n+1} - a_n} - 1 &= \frac{1}{n + \frac{2}{3}}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \left(5^{a_{n+1}-a_n}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{5}\right) &= \frac{1}{n+\frac{2}{3}}\cdot\frac{3}{3}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{4}{5}5^{a_{n+1}-a_n} &= \frac{3}{3n+2}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad 5^{a_{n+1}-a_n} &= \frac{15}{4}\cdot\frac{1}{(3n+2)/3}
\end{align*}

Now, we need to find the least $k$ such that $\prod_{n=1}^{k-1} 5^{a_{n+1}-a_n}$ is an integer. Thus,

$$\frac{15^k}{4^k}\cdot\frac{3}{(3k+2)/3}=\frac{3\cdot5^k}{4^k}\cdot\frac{3^k}{(3k+2)^k}.$$Since $a_k$ is an integer, $\prod_{n=1}^{k-1} 5^{a_{n+1}-a_n}$ must be a power of $5$, so it follows that $5^k/(4k)^k$ must be a power of $5$. Therefore, the least value of $k$ is given by $$k=\boxed{243}$$

